{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Check if in the given list of numbers, any two numbers are closer to each other\n    than the given threshold.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers (List[float]): A list of floating-point numbers.\n        threshold (float): The maximum absolute difference for two numbers to be\n                           considered \"close\". Must be non-negative.\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if any two distinct numbers in the list are closer than the\n              threshold, False otherwise.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.0, 3.0], 0.5)\n        False\n        >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.8, 3.0, 4.0, 5.0, 2.0], 0.3)\n        True\n        >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 1.0001], 0.001)\n        True\n        >>> has_close_elements([], 0.1)\n        False\n        >>> has_close_elements([5.0], 0.1)\n        False\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        # Since the list is sorted, sorted_numbers[i+1] >= sorted_numbers[i],\n        # so the difference is non-negative and abs() is not needed.\n        if (sorted_numbers[i+1] - sorted_numbers[i]) < threshold:\n            return True\n\n    return False"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/1", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Separates distinct, balanced, and non-nested groups of parentheses from an\n    input string.\n\n    The function takes a string containing parenthesis characters and spaces,\n    removes all space characters, and identifies top-level, balanced\n    parenthesis groups. Each identified group is returned as a string in a list,\n    preserving their original order.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string (str): The input string containing parentheses and spaces.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of strings, where each string represents a separated,\n                   balanced, and non-nested group of parentheses. If no valid\n                   groups are found, an empty list is returned.\n\n    Example:\n        >>> separate_paren_groups(\"(()()) ((())) ()\")\n        ['(()())', '((()))', '()']\n        >>> separate_paren_groups(\"() ()\")\n        ['()', '()']\n        >>> separate_paren_groups(\"((()))\")\n        ['((()))']\n        >>> separate_paren_groups(\"\")\n        []\n        >>> separate_paren_groups(\"   \")\n        []\n    \"\"\"\n    result: List[str] = []\n    current_group_chars: List[str] = []\n    balance: int = 0\n\n    for char in paren_string:\n        if char == ' ':\n            continue\n\n        current_group_chars.append(char)\n\n        if char == '(':\n            balance += 1\n        elif char == ')':\n            balance -= 1\n\n        # A complete top-level group is found when the balance returns to 0\n        # and there are characters accumulated in current_group_chars.\n        # This implicitly handles the \"non-nested\" requirement for the output list\n        # because we only capture groups when their top-level balance is zero.\n        if balance == 0 and current_group_chars:\n            result.append(\"\".join(current_group_chars))\n            current_group_chars = [] # Reset for the next group\n\n    return result"}
