{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimeter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Insert a number 'delimeter' between every two consecutive elements of input list `numbers'\n    >>> intersperse([], 4)\n    []\n    >>> intersperse([1, 2, 3], 4)\n    [1, 4, 2, 4, 3]\n    \"\"\"\n    result_list = []\n    n = len(numbers)\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        result_list.append(numbers[i])\n        if i < n - 1:\n            result_list.append(delimeter)\n\n    return result_list"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef _calculate_max_nesting(group_str: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum nesting level of parentheses for a single group string.\n\n    Args:\n        group_str: A string consisting only of '(' and ')' characters, representing\n                   a single group of parentheses.\n\n    Returns:\n        The maximum nesting level encountered in the group_str.\n    \"\"\"\n    current_nesting = 0\n    max_nesting = 0\n    for char in group_str:\n        if char == '(':\n            current_nesting += 1\n            max_nesting = max(max_nesting, current_nesting)\n        elif char == ')':\n            current_nesting -= 1\n    return max_nesting\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string containing multiple space-separated groups of parentheses\n    and calculates the maximum nesting level for each group.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string: A string where individual groups of parentheses are\n                      separated by one or more spaces. Each group is assumed\n                      to consist only of '(' and ')' characters.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of integers, where each integer is the maximum nesting level\n        for its corresponding group in the input string. Returns an empty\n        list if the input string is empty or contains only whitespace.\n    \"\"\"\n    results: List[int] = []\n\n    # Split the string by whitespace.\n    # str.split() without arguments handles multiple spaces and leading/trailing\n    # spaces by discarding empty strings, fulfilling FR-2 and FR-5 implicitly.\n    groups = paren_string.split()\n\n    for group_str in groups:\n        max_level = _calculate_max_nesting(group_str)\n        results.append(max_level)\n\n    return results"}
